# Part 10b (ADR-0332 items 7 and 8, ADR-0383 to ADR-0385): text selected with
# the mouse in a terminal is copied with System+C and pasted at the prompt with
# System+V, where it waits for Enter; a paste with a line end makes
# continuation lines and runs nothing; the keys F5, F1, Alt+x and Ctrl+Right
# send the sequences the entry names, shown at the prompt by Ctrl+V. Rows are 18
# logical pixels high from y 120 (the first row's middle is 129), the text
# starts at x 87 and a cell is 8 wide, whatever the display's size and scale;
# a large display has fewer rows (22 at 1920 x 1200), so the script clears the
# screen first and uses its first rows only. An empty row is term.bg, ffffff.
serial init: gui: gpu on its own page
screen Type help to begin, or help tour.
screen ~> _
chord ctrl l
top ~> _
type echo pasted text works\r
screen pasted text works
screen ~> _
# Row 0 reads `~> echo pasted text works`: the command is cells 3 to 24, x 111
# to 286. The drag runs on to cell 60 (x 571): a press, a pause (a frame between
# the press and the move), the move and the release. The cells 26 to 29 hold
# nothing and are drawn selected (bg.selection, dfe4ec); what is copied ends at
# the last letter.
drag 112 129 571 129 400
region 296 121 32 16 dfe4ec
# System+C offers it, System+V pastes it: at the prompt, row 2, not run.
chord meta c
pause 300
chord meta v
window
screen ~> echo pasted text works_
region 87 174 640 18 ffffff
pause 800
region 87 174 640 18 ffffff
# Enter is the person's: now it runs.
type \r
window
screen ~> echo pasted text works
screen pasted text works
screen ~> _
# A selection across two rows, pasted with its line end: continuation lines.
# The pointer of a drag stays where it ends, drawn over the two rows it is on, and
# the reader sees it as a glyph there: the pointer is parked, by a wheel turn that
# changes nothing (there is no scrollback), below the rows the script reads, and
# the second drag ends on rows the script reads no more.
wheel 650 480 down 1
chord ctrl l
window
type echo echo A; echo echo B\r
screen echo A
screen echo B
screen ~> _
# Rows 1 and 2: `echo A` is cells 0 to 5 of row 1 (y 147), `echo B` of row 2
# (y 165). The drag goes backwards, from cell 60 of row 2 (blanks, which are not
# copied) to the first cell of row 1: the same selection, and the pointer ends
# over rows 1 and 2, which are not read again.
drag 571 165 88 147 400
chord meta c
pause 300
chord meta v
window
screen ~> echo A
screen ...> echo B_
region 87 210 640 18 ffffff
pause 800
region 87 210 640 18 ffffff
type \r
window
screen ...> echo B
screen A
screen B
screen ~> _
# Function keys, Alt and Ctrl+arrows as the terminal sends them, shown by
# Ctrl+V at the prompt: F5 is CSI 15 ~, Ctrl+Right CSI 1;5 C, Alt+x ESC x, F1 SS3 P.
chord ctrl l
top ~> _
chord ctrl v
key f5
chord ctrl v
chord ctrl right
chord ctrl v
chord alt x
chord ctrl v
key f1
top ~> ^[[15~^[[1;5C^[x^[OP_
chord ctrl u
top ~> _
